{"authored" [] "sources" [{"question" "q-119760" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The mass $M(t),$ in micrograms, of a sample of manganese-53 is given by\n\n$$\nM(t)=17{e}^{-0.187t},\n$$\n\nwhere $t$ is the time, in millions of years. Rounded to one decimal place, what is the half-life of manganese-53?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14047446/state.json" "file_sha256" "2683497908ddd9ff288e40508eaa66744ec80d4c4c87fb7e4bd164574ef440ed"} {"question" "q-119760" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The mass $M(t)$ of a radioactive substance at time $t$ can be modeled by the differential equation\n\n$$\n{M}^{′}(t)=-kM(t),\\,M(0)={M}_{0}.\n$$\n\nThe general solution to this equation is\n\n$$\nM(t)={M}_{0}{e}^{-kt}.\n$$\n\nThe half-life ${t}_{1/2}$ is the time taken for the mass of the substance to reduce by one half and is given by\n\n$$\n{t}_{1/2}=\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{k}.\n$$\n\nWe're given that $k=0.187.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{t}_{1/2} & =\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{k} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{0.187} \\\\  & ≈3.7\\,\\text{million years}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14047446/state.json" "file_sha256" "2683497908ddd9ff288e40508eaa66744ec80d4c4c87fb7e4bd164574ef440ed"} {"question" "q-119760" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14047446/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "6c55a0b8ef2d290be1baf4020ae915a1985f32a1a26249515158e73e63f0c004"} {"question" "q-119760" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14047446/state.json" "file_sha256" "2683497908ddd9ff288e40508eaa66744ec80d4c4c87fb7e4bd164574ef440ed"} {"question" "q-119760" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "$12.8$ million years"] ["text" "$20.0$ million years"] ["text" "$3.7$ million years"] ["text" "$5.5$ million years"] ["text" "$8.0$ million years"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14047446/state.json" "file_sha256" "2683497908ddd9ff288e40508eaa66744ec80d4c4c87fb7e4bd164574ef440ed"} {"question" "q-119760" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "$3.7$ million years"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14047446/state.json" "file_sha256" "2683497908ddd9ff288e40508eaa66744ec80d4c4c87fb7e4bd164574ef440ed"} {"question" "q-355590" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The mass $M(t),$ in micrograms, of a sample of neon-19 is given by\n\n$$\nM(t)=4.5{e}^{-kt},\n$$\n\nwhere $t$ is the time, in seconds. The half-life of neon-19 is $17.2\\,\\text{s}.$ Rounded to **three significant figures**, what is the value of $k?$\n\n$k≈$\n{{field-1}} ${\\text{s}}^{-1}$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14047446/state.json" "file_sha256" "2683497908ddd9ff288e40508eaa66744ec80d4c4c87fb7e4bd164574ef440ed"} {"question" "q-355590" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The mass $M(t)$ of a radioactive substance at time $t$ can be modeled by the differential equation\n\n$$\n{M}^{′}(t)=-kM(t),\\,M(0)={M}_{0}.\n$$\n\nThe general solution to this equation is\n\n$$\nM(t)={M}_{0}{e}^{-kt}.\n$$\n\nThe half-life ${t}_{1/2}$ is the time taken for the mass of the substance to reduce by one half and is given by\n\n$$\n{t}_{1/2}=\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{k}.\n$$\n\nSince we know that ${t}_{1/2}=17.2\\,\\text{s},$ we have\n\n$$\n17.2=\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{k}\\,⇒\\,k=\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{17.2}≈0.0403\\,{\\text{s}}^{-1}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14047446/state.json" "file_sha256" "2683497908ddd9ff288e40508eaa66744ec80d4c4c87fb7e4bd164574ef440ed"} {"question" "q-355590" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14047446/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "6c55a0b8ef2d290be1baf4020ae915a1985f32a1a26249515158e73e63f0c004"} {"question" "q-355590" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14047446/state.json" "file_sha256" "2683497908ddd9ff288e40508eaa66744ec80d4c4c87fb7e4bd164574ef440ed"} {"question" "q-355590" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "0.0403"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14047446/state.json" "file_sha256" "2683497908ddd9ff288e40508eaa66744ec80d4c4c87fb7e4bd164574ef440ed"} {"question" "q-118595" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The mass $M(t),$ in micrograms, of a sample of curium-240 is given by\n\n$$\nM(t)=8{e}^{-0.025\\,67t},\n$$\n\nwhere $t$ is the time, in days. Rounded to one decimal place, what is the half-life of curium-240?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14047446/state.json" "file_sha256" "2683497908ddd9ff288e40508eaa66744ec80d4c4c87fb7e4bd164574ef440ed"} {"question" "q-118595" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The mass $M(t)$ of a radioactive substance at time $t$ can be modeled by the differential equation\n\n$$\n{M}^{′}(t)=-kM(t),\\,M(0)={M}_{0}.\n$$\n\nThe general solution to this equation is\n\n$$\nM(t)={M}_{0}{e}^{-kt}.\n$$\n\nThe half-life ${t}_{1/2}$ is the time taken for the mass of the substance to reduce by one half and is given by\n\n$$\n{t}_{1/2}=\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{k}.\n$$\n\nWe're given that $k=0.025\\,67.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{t}_{1/2} & =\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{k} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{0.025\\,67} \\\\  & ≈27.0\\,\\text{days}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14047446/state.json" "file_sha256" "2683497908ddd9ff288e40508eaa66744ec80d4c4c87fb7e4bd164574ef440ed"} {"question" "q-118595" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14047446/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "6c55a0b8ef2d290be1baf4020ae915a1985f32a1a26249515158e73e63f0c004"} {"question" "q-118595" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14047446/state.json" "file_sha256" "2683497908ddd9ff288e40508eaa66744ec80d4c4c87fb7e4bd164574ef440ed"} {"question" "q-118595" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "$25.2$ days"] ["text" "$26.6$ days"] ["text" "$27.0$ days"] ["text" "$27.4$ days"] ["text" "$27.8$ days"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14047446/state.json" "file_sha256" "2683497908ddd9ff288e40508eaa66744ec80d4c4c87fb7e4bd164574ef440ed"} {"question" "q-118595" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "$27.0$ days"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14047446/state.json" "file_sha256" "2683497908ddd9ff288e40508eaa66744ec80d4c4c87fb7e4bd164574ef440ed"} {"question" "q-15588" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The mass $M(t),$ in micrograms, of a sample of potassium-42 is given by\n\n$$\nM(t)=30{e}^{-kt},\n$$\n\nwhere $t$ is the time, in hours. The half-life of potassium-42 is $12.36\\,\\text{h}.$ Rounded to three significant figures, what is the value of $k?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14047446/state.json" "file_sha256" "2683497908ddd9ff288e40508eaa66744ec80d4c4c87fb7e4bd164574ef440ed"} {"question" "q-15588" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The mass $M(t)$ of a radioactive substance at time $t$ can be modeled by the differential equation\n\n$$\n{M}^{′}(t)=-kM(t),\\,M(0)={M}_{0}.\n$$\n\nThe general solution to this equation is\n\n$$\nM(t)={M}_{0}{e}^{-kt}.\n$$\n\nThe half-life ${t}_{1/2}$ is the time taken for the mass of the substance to reduce by one half and is given by\n\n$$\n{t}_{1/2}=\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{k}.\n$$\n\nSince we know that ${t}_{1/2}=12.36\\,\\text{h}$ we have\n\n$$\n12.36=\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{k}\\,⇒\\,k=\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{12.36}≈0.0561\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14047446/state.json" "file_sha256" "2683497908ddd9ff288e40508eaa66744ec80d4c4c87fb7e4bd164574ef440ed"} {"question" "q-15588" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14047446/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "6c55a0b8ef2d290be1baf4020ae915a1985f32a1a26249515158e73e63f0c004"} {"question" "q-15588" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14047446/state.json" "file_sha256" "2683497908ddd9ff288e40508eaa66744ec80d4c4c87fb7e4bd164574ef440ed"} {"question" "q-15588" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "0.0157"] ["math" "0.0561"] ["math" "0.153"] ["math" "0.433"] ["math" "1.35"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14047446/state.json" "file_sha256" "2683497908ddd9ff288e40508eaa66744ec80d4c4c87fb7e4bd164574ef440ed"} {"question" "q-15588" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "0.0561"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14047446/state.json" "file_sha256" "2683497908ddd9ff288e40508eaa66744ec80d4c4c87fb7e4bd164574ef440ed"} {"question" "q-118593" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The mass $M(t),$ in micrograms, of a sample of rhodium-101 is given by\n\n$$\nM(t)=7{e}^{-0.21t},\n$$\n\nwhere $t$ is the time, in years. Rounded to one decimal place, what is the half-life of rhodium-101?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14047446/state.json" "file_sha256" "2683497908ddd9ff288e40508eaa66744ec80d4c4c87fb7e4bd164574ef440ed"} {"question" "q-118593" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The mass $M(t)$ of a radioactive substance at time $t$ can be modeled by the differential equation\n\n$$\n{M}^{′}(t)=-kM(t),\\,M(0)={M}_{0}.\n$$\n\nThe general solution to this equation is\n\n$$\nM(t)={M}_{0}{e}^{-kt}.\n$$\n\nThe half-life ${t}_{1/2}$ is the time taken for the mass of the substance to reduce by one half and is given by\n\n$$\n{t}_{1/2}=\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{k}.\n$$\n\nWe're given that $k=0.21.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{t}_{1/2} & =\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{k} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{0.21} \\\\  & ≈3.3\\,\\text{years}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14047446/state.json" "file_sha256" "2683497908ddd9ff288e40508eaa66744ec80d4c4c87fb7e4bd164574ef440ed"} {"question" "q-118593" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14047446/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "6c55a0b8ef2d290be1baf4020ae915a1985f32a1a26249515158e73e63f0c004"} {"question" "q-118593" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14047446/state.json" "file_sha256" "2683497908ddd9ff288e40508eaa66744ec80d4c4c87fb7e4bd164574ef440ed"} {"question" "q-118593" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "$2.9$ years"] ["text" "$3.1$ years"] ["text" "$3.3$ years"] ["text" "$3.7$ years"] ["text" "$4.2$ years"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14047446/state.json" "file_sha256" "2683497908ddd9ff288e40508eaa66744ec80d4c4c87fb7e4bd164574ef440ed"} {"question" "q-118593" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "$3.3$ years"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14047446/state.json" "file_sha256" "2683497908ddd9ff288e40508eaa66744ec80d4c4c87fb7e4bd164574ef440ed"} {"question" "q-353546" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The half-life of polonium-210 is $138.4$ days. How long does it take for a $8.8\\,\\mu \\text{g}$ sample of polonium-210 to reduce to $1.1\\,\\mu \\text{g}?$ Do not round the answer.\n\n$t=$\n{{field-1}}\n$\\text{days}$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14047446/state.json" "file_sha256" "2683497908ddd9ff288e40508eaa66744ec80d4c4c87fb7e4bd164574ef440ed"} {"question" "q-353546" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The mass $M(t)$ of a radioactive substance at time $t$ can be modeled by the differential equation\n\n$$\n{M}^{′}(t)=-kM(t),\\,M(0)={M}_{0}.\n$$\n\nThe general solution to this equation is\n\n$$\nM(t)={M}_{0}{e}^{-kt}.\n$$\n\nThe half-life ${t}_{1/2}$ is the time taken for the mass of the substance to reduce by one half and is given by\n\n$$\n{t}_{1/2}=\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{k}.\n$$\n\nThere are two methods that we can use to solve this problem. We describe each of them below.\n\n**Method 1**\n\nSince we know that ${t}_{1/2}=138.4$ days, we have\n\n$$\n138.4=\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{k}\\,⇒\\,k=\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{138.4}\n$$\n\nAlso, since ${M}_{0}=8.8\\,\\mu \\text{g},$ we have the particular solution\n\n$$\nM(t)=8.8{e}^{-t\\operatorname{ln}⁡2/138.4}.\n$$\n\nWe wish to calculate the time $t$ such that $M(t)=1.1\\,\\mu \\text{g}.$ So, we set up our equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1.1 & =8.8{e}^{-t\\operatorname{ln}⁡2/138.4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this equation for $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1.1 & =8.8{e}^{-t\\operatorname{ln}⁡2/138.4} \\\\ \\frac{1.1}{8.8} & ={e}^{-t\\operatorname{ln}⁡2/138.4} \\\\ \\frac{1}{8} & ={e}^{-t\\operatorname{ln}⁡2/138.4} \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{1}{8}) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{-t\\operatorname{ln}⁡2/138.4}) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡1-\\operatorname{ln}⁡8 & =-\\frac{t\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{138.4} \\\\ -\\operatorname{ln}⁡8 & =-\\frac{t\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{138.4} \\\\ t & =\\frac{138.4\\cdot \\operatorname{ln}⁡8}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2} \\\\ t & =\\frac{138.4\\cdot 3\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2} \\\\ t & =415.2\\,\\text{days}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Method 2**\n\nWe wish to calculate the time $t$ such that $M(t)=1.1\\,\\mu \\text{g}.$ Notice that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{M(t)}{M(0)} & =\\frac{1.1\\,\\mu \\text{g}}{8.8\\,\\mu \\text{g}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8} \\\\  & ={(\\frac{1}{2})}^{3},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nor equivalently\n\n$$\nM(t)={(\\frac{1}{2})}^{3}\\cdot M(0).\n$$\n\nFrom this equation, we see that $3$ half-lives must have occurred for the mass to reduce from $8.8\\,\\mu \\text{g}$ to $1.1\\,\\mu \\text{g}.$ Hence,\n\n$$\nt=3\\cdot {t}_{1/2}=3\\cdot 138.4=415.2\\,\\text{days}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14047446/state.json" "file_sha256" "2683497908ddd9ff288e40508eaa66744ec80d4c4c87fb7e4bd164574ef440ed"} {"question" "q-353546" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14047446/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "6c55a0b8ef2d290be1baf4020ae915a1985f32a1a26249515158e73e63f0c004"} {"question" "q-353546" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14047446/state.json" "file_sha256" "2683497908ddd9ff288e40508eaa66744ec80d4c4c87fb7e4bd164574ef440ed"} {"question" "q-353546" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "415.2"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14047446/state.json" "file_sha256" "2683497908ddd9ff288e40508eaa66744ec80d4c4c87fb7e4bd164574ef440ed"} {"question" "q-120006" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The half-life of astatine-223 is $50\\,\\text{s}.$ How long does it take for an $400\\,\\mu \\text{g}$ sample of astatine-223 to reduce to $50\\,\\mu \\text{g}?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14047446/state.json" "file_sha256" "2683497908ddd9ff288e40508eaa66744ec80d4c4c87fb7e4bd164574ef440ed"} {"question" "q-120006" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The mass $M(t)$ of a radioactive substance at time $t$ can be modeled by the differential equation\n\n$$\n{M}^{′}(t)=-kM(t),\\,M(0)={M}_{0}.\n$$\n\nThe general solution to this equation is\n\n$$\nM(t)={M}_{0}{e}^{-kt}.\n$$\n\nThe half-life ${t}_{1/2}$ is the time taken for the mass of the substance to reduce by one half and is given by\n\n$$\n{t}_{1/2}=\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{k}.\n$$\n\nThere are two methods that we can use to solve this problem. We describe each of them below.\n\n**Method 1**\n\nSince we know that ${t}_{1/2}=50\\,\\text{s},$ we have\n\n$$\n50=\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{k}\\,⇒\\,k=\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{50}\n$$\n\nAlso, since ${M}_{0}=400\\,\\mu \\text{g},$ we have the particular solution\n\n$$\nM(t)=400{e}^{-t\\operatorname{ln}⁡2/50}.\n$$\n\nWe wish to calculate the time $t$ such that $M(t)=50\\,\\mu \\text{g}.$ So, we set up our equation as follows:\n\n$$\n50=400{e}^{-t\\operatorname{ln}⁡2/50}\\,\\,\n$$\n\nSolving this equation for $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}50 & =400{e}^{-t\\operatorname{ln}⁡2/50} \\\\ \\frac{50}{400} & ={e}^{-t\\operatorname{ln}⁡2/50} \\\\ \\frac{1}{8} & ={e}^{-t\\operatorname{ln}⁡2/50} \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{1}{8}) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{-t\\operatorname{ln}⁡2/50}) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡1-\\operatorname{ln}⁡8 & =-\\frac{t\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{50} \\\\ -\\operatorname{ln}⁡8 & =-\\frac{t\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{50} \\\\ t & =\\frac{50\\cdot \\operatorname{ln}⁡8}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2} \\\\ t & =150.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that it takes $150\\,\\text{s}$ for the mass of the substance to reduce to $50\\,\\mu \\text{g}.$\n\n**Method 2**\n\nWe wish to calculate the time $t$ such that $M(t)=50\\,\\mu \\text{g}.$ Notice that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{M(t)}{M(0)} & =\\frac{50\\,\\mu \\text{g}}{400\\,\\mu \\text{g}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8} \\\\  & ={(\\frac{1}{2})}^{3},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nor equivalently\n\n$$\nM(t)={(\\frac{1}{2})}^{3}\\cdot M(0).\n$$\n\nFrom this equation, we see that $3$ half-lives must have occurred for the mass to reduce from $400\\,\\mu \\text{g}$ to $50\\,\\mu \\text{g}.$ Hence,\n\n$$\nt=3\\cdot {t}_{1/2}=3\\cdot 50\\,\\text{s}=150\\,\\text{s}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that it takes $150\\,\\text{s}$ for the mass of the substance to reduce to $50\\,\\mu \\text{g}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14047446/state.json" "file_sha256" "2683497908ddd9ff288e40508eaa66744ec80d4c4c87fb7e4bd164574ef440ed"} {"question" "q-120006" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14047446/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "6c55a0b8ef2d290be1baf4020ae915a1985f32a1a26249515158e73e63f0c004"} {"question" "q-120006" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14047446/state.json" "file_sha256" "2683497908ddd9ff288e40508eaa66744ec80d4c4c87fb7e4bd164574ef440ed"} {"question" "q-120006" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "100\\,\\text{s}"] ["math" "150\\,\\text{s}"] ["math" "200\\,\\text{s}"] ["math" "300\\,\\text{s}"] ["math" "75\\,\\text{s}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14047446/state.json" "file_sha256" "2683497908ddd9ff288e40508eaa66744ec80d4c4c87fb7e4bd164574ef440ed"} {"question" "q-120006" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "150\\,\\text{s}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14047446/state.json" "file_sha256" "2683497908ddd9ff288e40508eaa66744ec80d4c4c87fb7e4bd164574ef440ed"} {"question" "q-353464" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Iodine-131 has a half-life of $8.02$ days. A sample of iodine-131 has an initial mass of $7.2\\,\\mu \\text{g}.$ Rounded to **one decimal place**, what is the mass of the sample after $12$ days?\n\nThe mass of the sample after $12$ days is approximately\n{{field-1}}\n$\\mu \\text{g}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14047446/state.json" "file_sha256" "2683497908ddd9ff288e40508eaa66744ec80d4c4c87fb7e4bd164574ef440ed"} {"question" "q-353464" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The mass $M(t)$ of a radioactive substance at time $t$ can be modeled by the differential equation\n\n$$\n{M}^{′}(t)=-kM(t),\\,M(0)={M}_{0}.\n$$\n\nThe general solution to this equation is\n\n$$\nM(t)={M}_{0}{e}^{-kt}.\n$$\n\nThe half-life ${t}_{1/2}$ is the time taken for the mass of the substance to reduce by one half and is given by\n\n$$\n{t}_{1/2}=\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{k}.\n$$\n\nSince we know that ${t}_{1/2}=8.02$ days, we have\n\n$$\n8.02=\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{k}\\,⇒\\,k=\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{8.02}.\n$$\n\nAlso, since ${M}_{0}=7.2\\,\\mu \\text{g},$ we have the particular solution\n\n$$\nM(t)=7.2{e}^{-t\\operatorname{ln}⁡2/8.02}.\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\nM(12)=7.2{e}^{-12\\operatorname{ln}⁡2/8.02}≈2.6,\n$$\n\nrounded to one decimal place.\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the mass of the sample remaining after $12$ days is approximately $2.6\\,\\mu \\text{g}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14047446/state.json" "file_sha256" "2683497908ddd9ff288e40508eaa66744ec80d4c4c87fb7e4bd164574ef440ed"} {"question" "q-353464" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14047446/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "6c55a0b8ef2d290be1baf4020ae915a1985f32a1a26249515158e73e63f0c004"} {"question" "q-353464" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14047446/state.json" "file_sha256" "2683497908ddd9ff288e40508eaa66744ec80d4c4c87fb7e4bd164574ef440ed"} {"question" "q-353464" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "2.6"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14047446/state.json" "file_sha256" "2683497908ddd9ff288e40508eaa66744ec80d4c4c87fb7e4bd164574ef440ed"} {"question" "q-347510" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Aluminum-26 has a half-life of $0.717$ million years. A sample of aluminum-26 has an initial mass of $40\\,\\mu \\text{g}.$ Rounded to **one decimal place**, what is the mass of the sample after $1$ million years?\n\nThe mass of the sample after $1$ million years is approximately\n{{field-1}}\n$\\mu \\text{g}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14047446/state.json" "file_sha256" "2683497908ddd9ff288e40508eaa66744ec80d4c4c87fb7e4bd164574ef440ed"} {"question" "q-347510" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The mass $M(t)$ of a radioactive substance at time $t$ can be modeled by the differential equation\n\n$$\n{M}^{′}(t)=-kM(t),\\,M(0)={M}_{0}.\n$$\n\nThe general solution to this equation is\n\n$$\nM(t)={M}_{0}{e}^{-kt}.\n$$\n\nThe half-life ${t}_{1/2}$ is the time taken for the mass of the substance to reduce by one half and is given by\n\n$$\n{t}_{1/2}=\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{k}.\n$$\n\nSince we know that ${t}_{1/2}=0.717$ million years, we have\n\n$$\n0.717=\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{k}\\,⇒\\,k=\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{0.717}.\n$$\n\nAlso, since ${M}_{0}=40\\,\\mu \\text{g},$ we have the particular solution\n\n$$\nM(t)=40{e}^{-t\\operatorname{ln}⁡2/0.717}.\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\nM(1)=40{e}^{-\\operatorname{ln}⁡2/0.717}≈15.2,\n$$\n\nrounded to one decimal place.\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the mass of the sample remaining after $1$ million years is approximately $15.2\\,\\mu \\text{g}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14047446/state.json" "file_sha256" "2683497908ddd9ff288e40508eaa66744ec80d4c4c87fb7e4bd164574ef440ed"} {"question" "q-347510" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14047446/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "6c55a0b8ef2d290be1baf4020ae915a1985f32a1a26249515158e73e63f0c004"} {"question" "q-347510" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14047446/state.json" "file_sha256" "2683497908ddd9ff288e40508eaa66744ec80d4c4c87fb7e4bd164574ef440ed"} {"question" "q-347510" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "15.2"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14047446/state.json" "file_sha256" "2683497908ddd9ff288e40508eaa66744ec80d4c4c87fb7e4bd164574ef440ed"} {"question" "q-118602" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The half-life of francium-224 is $3.33\\,\\text{min}.$ Rounded to the nearest minute, how long does it take for a $160\\,\\mu \\text{g}$ sample of francium-224 to reduce to $20\\,\\mu \\text{g}?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14047446/state.json" "file_sha256" "2683497908ddd9ff288e40508eaa66744ec80d4c4c87fb7e4bd164574ef440ed"} {"question" "q-118602" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The mass $M(t)$ of a radioactive substance at time $t$ can be modeled by the differential equation\n\n$$\n{M}^{′}(t)=-kM(t),\\,M(0)={M}_{0}.\n$$\n\nThe general solution to this equation is\n\n$$\nM(t)={M}_{0}{e}^{-kt}.\n$$\n\nThe half-life ${t}_{1/2}$ is the time taken for the mass of the substance to reduce by one half and is given by\n\n$$\n{t}_{1/2}=\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{k}.\n$$\n\nThere are two methods that we can use to solve this problem. We describe each of them below.\n\n**Method 1**\n\nSince we know that ${t}_{1/2}=3.33\\,\\text{min},$ we have\n\n$$\n3.33=\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{k}\\,⇒\\,k=\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{3.33}\n$$\n\nAlso, since ${M}_{0}=160\\,\\mu \\text{g},$ we have the particular solution\n\n$$\nM(t)=160{e}^{-t\\operatorname{ln}⁡2/3.33}.\n$$\n\nWe wish to calculate the time $t$ such that $M(t)=20\\,\\mu \\text{g}.$ So, we set up our equation as follows:\n\n$$\n20=160{e}^{-t\\operatorname{ln}⁡2/3.33}\\,\\,\n$$\n\nSolving this equation for $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}20 & =160{e}^{-t\\operatorname{ln}⁡2/3.33} \\\\ \\frac{20}{160} & ={e}^{-t\\operatorname{ln}⁡2/3.33} \\\\ \\frac{1}{8} & ={e}^{-t\\operatorname{ln}⁡2/3.33} \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{1}{8}) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{-t\\operatorname{ln}⁡2/3.33}) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡1-\\operatorname{ln}⁡8 & =-\\frac{t\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{3.33} \\\\ -\\operatorname{ln}⁡8 & =-\\frac{t\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{3.33} \\\\ t & =\\frac{3.33\\cdot \\operatorname{ln}⁡8}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2} \\\\ t & ≈10.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that it takes $10\\,\\text{min}$ for the mass of the substance to reduce to $20\\,\\mu \\text{g}.$\n\n**Method 2**\n\nWe wish to calculate the time $t$ such that $M(t)=20\\,\\mu \\text{g}.$ Notice that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{M(t)}{M(0)} & =\\frac{20\\,\\mu \\text{g}}{160\\,\\mu \\text{g}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8} \\\\  & ={(\\frac{1}{2})}^{3},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nor equivalently\n\n$$\nM(t)={(\\frac{1}{2})}^{3}\\cdot M(0).\n$$\n\nFrom this equation, we see that $3$ half-lives must have occurred for the mass to reduce from $160\\,\\mu \\text{g}$ to $20\\,\\mu \\text{g}.$ Hence,\n\n$$\nt=3\\cdot {t}_{1/2}=3\\cdot 3.33\\,\\text{min}≈10\\,\\text{min}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that it takes $10\\,\\text{min}$ for the mass of the substance to reduce to $20\\,\\mu \\text{g}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14047446/state.json" "file_sha256" "2683497908ddd9ff288e40508eaa66744ec80d4c4c87fb7e4bd164574ef440ed"} {"question" "q-118602" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14047446/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "6c55a0b8ef2d290be1baf4020ae915a1985f32a1a26249515158e73e63f0c004"} {"question" "q-118602" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14047446/state.json" "file_sha256" "2683497908ddd9ff288e40508eaa66744ec80d4c4c87fb7e4bd164574ef440ed"} {"question" "q-118602" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "10\\,\\text{min}"] ["math" "13\\,\\text{min}"] ["math" "15\\,\\text{min}"] ["math" "7\\,\\text{min}"] ["math" "8\\,\\text{min}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14047446/state.json" "file_sha256" "2683497908ddd9ff288e40508eaa66744ec80d4c4c87fb7e4bd164574ef440ed"} {"question" "q-118602" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "10\\,\\text{min}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14047446/state.json" "file_sha256" "2683497908ddd9ff288e40508eaa66744ec80d4c4c87fb7e4bd164574ef440ed"} {"question" "q-118723" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "At a geological site, a sample of a lunar rock is found with $99%$ of its original rubidium-87 content. Given that the half-life of rubidium-87 is $49.72$ billion years, approximately how old is the sample?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14047446/state.json" "file_sha256" "2683497908ddd9ff288e40508eaa66744ec80d4c4c87fb7e4bd164574ef440ed"} {"question" "q-118723" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The mass $M(t)$ of a radioactive substance at time $t$ can be modeled by the differential equation\n\n$$\n{M}^{′}(t)=-kM(t),\\,M(0)={M}_{0}.\n$$\n\nThe general solution to this equation is\n\n$$\nM(t)={M}_{0}{e}^{-kt}.\n$$\n\nThe half-life ${t}_{1/2}$ is the time taken for the mass of the substance to reduce by one half and is given by\n\n$$\n{t}_{1/2}=\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{k}.\n$$\n\nSince we know that ${t}_{1/2}=49.72$ billion years, we have\n\n$$\n49.72=\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{k}\\,⇒\\,k=\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{49.72}.\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\nM(t)={M}_{0}{e}^{-t\\operatorname{ln}⁡2/49.72}.\n$$\n\nWe wish to calculate the time $t$ such that $M(t)=0.99{M}_{0}.$ So, we set up our equation as follows:\n\n$$\n0.99{M}_{0}={M}_{0}{e}^{-t\\operatorname{ln}⁡2/49.72}\n$$\n\nSolving this equation for $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0.99{M}_{0} & ={M}_{0}{e}^{-t\\operatorname{ln}⁡2/49.72} \\\\ 0.99\\cancel{{M}_{0}} & =\\cancel{{M}_{0}}{e}^{-t\\operatorname{ln}⁡2/49.72} \\\\ 0.99 & ={e}^{-t\\operatorname{ln}⁡2/49.72} \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡(0.99) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{-t\\operatorname{ln}⁡2/49.72}) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡(0.99) & =-\\frac{t\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{49.72} \\\\ t & =-\\frac{49.72\\cdot \\operatorname{ln}⁡(0.99)}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2} \\\\ t & ≈0.721\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the sample is approximately $0.721$ billion years old." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14047446/state.json" "file_sha256" "2683497908ddd9ff288e40508eaa66744ec80d4c4c87fb7e4bd164574ef440ed"} {"question" "q-118723" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14047446/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "6c55a0b8ef2d290be1baf4020ae915a1985f32a1a26249515158e73e63f0c004"} {"question" "q-118723" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14047446/state.json" "file_sha256" "2683497908ddd9ff288e40508eaa66744ec80d4c4c87fb7e4bd164574ef440ed"} {"question" "q-118723" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "$0.721$  billion years"] ["text" "$1.002$ billion years"] ["text" "$1.438$  billion years"] ["text" "$1.912$ billion years"] ["text" "$2.045$ billion years"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14047446/state.json" "file_sha256" "2683497908ddd9ff288e40508eaa66744ec80d4c4c87fb7e4bd164574ef440ed"} {"question" "q-118723" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "$0.721$  billion years"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14047446/state.json" "file_sha256" "2683497908ddd9ff288e40508eaa66744ec80d4c4c87fb7e4bd164574ef440ed"} {"question" "q-118727" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "At a geological site, an object is found with $95%$ of its original lutetium-176 content. Given that the half-life of lutetium-176 is $37.64$ billion years, approximately how old is the object?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14047446/state.json" "file_sha256" "2683497908ddd9ff288e40508eaa66744ec80d4c4c87fb7e4bd164574ef440ed"} {"question" "q-118727" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The mass $M(t)$ of a radioactive substance at time $t$ can be modeled by the differential equation\n\n$$\n{M}^{′}(t)=-kM(t),\\,M(0)={M}_{0}.\n$$\n\nThe general solution to this equation is\n\n$$\nM(t)={M}_{0}{e}^{-kt}.\n$$\n\nThe half-life ${t}_{1/2}$ is the time taken for the mass of the substance to reduce by one half and is given by\n\n$$\n{t}_{1/2}=\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{k}.\n$$\n\nSince we know that ${t}_{1/2}=37.64$ billion years, we have\n\n$$\n37.64=\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{k}\\,⇒\\,k=\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{37.64}.\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\nM(t)={M}_{0}{e}^{-t\\operatorname{ln}⁡2/37.64}.\n$$\n\nWe wish to calculate the time $t$ such that $M(t)=0.95{M}_{0}.$ So, we set up our equation as follows:\n\n$$\n0.95{M}_{0}={M}_{0}{e}^{-t\\operatorname{ln}⁡2/37.64}\n$$\n\nSolving this equation for $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0.95{M}_{0} & ={M}_{0}{e}^{-t\\operatorname{ln}⁡2/37.64} \\\\ 0.95\\cancel{{M}_{0}} & =\\cancel{{M}_{0}}{e}^{-t\\operatorname{ln}⁡2/37.64} \\\\ 0.95 & ={e}^{-t\\operatorname{ln}⁡2/37.64} \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡(0.95) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{-t\\operatorname{ln}⁡2/37.64}) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡(0.95) & =-\\frac{t\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{37.64} \\\\ t & =-\\frac{37.64\\cdot \\operatorname{ln}⁡(0.95)}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2} \\\\ t & ≈2.785\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the object is approximately $2.785$ billion years old." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14047446/state.json" "file_sha256" "2683497908ddd9ff288e40508eaa66744ec80d4c4c87fb7e4bd164574ef440ed"} {"question" "q-118727" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14047446/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "6c55a0b8ef2d290be1baf4020ae915a1985f32a1a26249515158e73e63f0c004"} {"question" "q-118727" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14047446/state.json" "file_sha256" "2683497908ddd9ff288e40508eaa66744ec80d4c4c87fb7e4bd164574ef440ed"} {"question" "q-118727" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "$0.721$ billion years"] ["text" "$1.002$ billion years"] ["text" "$1.438$  billion years"] ["text" "$2.045$ billion years"] ["text" "$2.785$  billion years"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14047446/state.json" "file_sha256" "2683497908ddd9ff288e40508eaa66744ec80d4c4c87fb7e4bd164574ef440ed"} {"question" "q-118727" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "$2.785$  billion years"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14047446/state.json" "file_sha256" "2683497908ddd9ff288e40508eaa66744ec80d4c4c87fb7e4bd164574ef440ed"} {"question" "q-15221" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "At an archaeological site, an object is found with $30%$ of its original carbon-14 content. Given that the half-life of carbon-14 is $5\\,730$ years, approximately how old is the object?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14047446/state.json" "file_sha256" "2683497908ddd9ff288e40508eaa66744ec80d4c4c87fb7e4bd164574ef440ed"} {"question" "q-15221" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The mass $M(t)$ of a radioactive substance at time $t$ can be modeled by the differential equation\n\n$$\n{M}^{′}(t)=-kM(t),\\,M(0)={M}_{0}.\n$$\n\nThe general solution to this equation is\n\n$$\nM(t)={M}_{0}{e}^{-kt}.\n$$\n\nThe half-life ${t}_{1/2}$ is the time taken for the mass of the substance to reduce by one half and is given by\n\n$$\n{t}_{1/2}=\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{k}.\n$$\n\nSince we know that ${t}_{1/2}=5\\,730$ years, we have\n\n$$\n5\\,730=\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{k}\\,⇒\\,k=\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{5\\,730}.\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\nM(t)={M}_{0}{e}^{-t\\operatorname{ln}⁡2/5\\,730}.\n$$\n\nWe wish to calculate the time $t$ such that $M(t)=0.3{M}_{0}.$ So, we set up our equation as follows:\n\n$$\n0.3{M}_{0}={M}_{0}{e}^{-t\\operatorname{ln}⁡2/5\\,730}\n$$\n\nSolving this equation for $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0.3{M}_{0} & ={M}_{0}{e}^{-t\\operatorname{ln}⁡2/5\\,730} \\\\ 0.3\\cancel{{M}_{0}} & =\\cancel{{M}_{0}}{e}^{-t\\operatorname{ln}⁡2/5\\,730} \\\\ 0.3 & ={e}^{-t\\operatorname{ln}⁡2/5\\,730} \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡(0.3) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{-t\\operatorname{ln}⁡2/5\\,730}) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡(0.3) & =-\\frac{t\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{5\\,730} \\\\ t & =-\\frac{5\\,730\\cdot \\operatorname{ln}⁡(0.3)}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2} \\\\ t & ≈9\\,953\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the object is approximately $9\\,953$ years old." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14047446/state.json" "file_sha256" "2683497908ddd9ff288e40508eaa66744ec80d4c4c87fb7e4bd164574ef440ed"} {"question" "q-15221" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14047446/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "6c55a0b8ef2d290be1baf4020ae915a1985f32a1a26249515158e73e63f0c004"} {"question" "q-15221" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14047446/state.json" "file_sha256" "2683497908ddd9ff288e40508eaa66744ec80d4c4c87fb7e4bd164574ef440ed"} {"question" "q-15221" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "$9\\,539$ years"] ["text" "$9\\,632$ years"] ["text" "$9\\,827$ years"] ["text" "$9\\,919$ years"] ["text" "$9\\,953$ years"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14047446/state.json" "file_sha256" "2683497908ddd9ff288e40508eaa66744ec80d4c4c87fb7e4bd164574ef440ed"} {"question" "q-15221" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "$9\\,953$ years"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14047446/state.json" "file_sha256" "2683497908ddd9ff288e40508eaa66744ec80d4c4c87fb7e4bd164574ef440ed"} {"question" "q-353610" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "In a medical imaging facility, a tracer is found with $25%$ of its original sodium-22 content. Given that the half-life of sodium-22 is $2.6$ years, how old is the tracer? Round your answer to **one decimal place**.\n\nThe tracer is approximately\n{{field-1}}\nyears old." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14047446/state.json" "file_sha256" "2683497908ddd9ff288e40508eaa66744ec80d4c4c87fb7e4bd164574ef440ed"} {"question" "q-353610" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The mass $M(t)$ of a radioactive substance at time $t$ can be modeled by the differential equation\n\n$$\n{M}^{′}(t)=-kM(t),\\,M(0)={M}_{0}.\n$$\n\nThe general solution to this equation is\n\n$$\nM(t)={M}_{0}{e}^{-kt}.\n$$\n\nThe half-life ${t}_{1/2}$ is the time taken for the mass of the substance to reduce by one half and is given by\n\n$$\n{t}_{1/2}=\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{k}.\n$$\n\nSince we know that ${t}_{1/2}=2.6$ years, we have\n\n$$\n2.6=\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{k}\\,⇒\\,k=\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{2.6}.\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\nM(t)={M}_{0}{e}^{-t\\operatorname{ln}⁡2/2.6}.\n$$\n\nWe wish to calculate the time $t$ such that $M(t)=0.25{M}_{0}.$ So, we set up our equation as follows:\n\n$$\n0.25{M}_{0}={M}_{0}{e}^{-t\\operatorname{ln}⁡2/2.6}\n$$\n\nSolving this equation for $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0.25{M}_{0} & ={M}_{0}{e}^{-t\\operatorname{ln}⁡2/2.6} \\\\ 0.25\\cancel{{M}_{0}} & =\\cancel{{M}_{0}}{e}^{-t\\operatorname{ln}⁡2/2.6} \\\\ 0.25 & ={e}^{-t\\operatorname{ln}⁡2/2.6} \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡(0.25) & =ln\\,({e}^{-t\\operatorname{ln}⁡2/2.6}) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡(0.25) & =-\\frac{t\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{2.6} \\\\ t & =-\\frac{2.6\\operatorname{ln}⁡(0.25)}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2} \\\\ t & ≈5.2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the tracer is approximately $5.2$ years old." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14047446/state.json" "file_sha256" "2683497908ddd9ff288e40508eaa66744ec80d4c4c87fb7e4bd164574ef440ed"} {"question" "q-353610" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14047446/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "6c55a0b8ef2d290be1baf4020ae915a1985f32a1a26249515158e73e63f0c004"} {"question" "q-353610" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14047446/state.json" "file_sha256" "2683497908ddd9ff288e40508eaa66744ec80d4c4c87fb7e4bd164574ef440ed"} {"question" "q-353610" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "5.2"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14047446/state.json" "file_sha256" "2683497908ddd9ff288e40508eaa66744ec80d4c4c87fb7e4bd164574ef440ed"} {"question" "q-355652" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "At a geologic study site, a sample is found with $42%$ of its original cesium-135 content. Given that the half-life of cesium-135 is $2.3$ million years, how old is the sample? Round your answer to **one decimal place**.\n\nThe sample is approximately\n{{field-1}}\nmillion years old." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14047446/state.json" "file_sha256" "2683497908ddd9ff288e40508eaa66744ec80d4c4c87fb7e4bd164574ef440ed"} {"question" "q-355652" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The mass $M(t)$ of a radioactive substance at time $t$ can be modeled by the differential equation\n\n$$\n{M}^{′}(t)=-kM(t),\\,M(0)={M}_{0}.\n$$\n\nThe general solution to this equation is\n\n$$\nM(t)={M}_{0}{e}^{-kt}.\n$$\n\nThe half-life ${t}_{1/2}$ is the time taken for the mass of the substance to reduce by one half and is given by\n\n$$\n{t}_{1/2}=\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{k}.\n$$\n\nSince we know that ${t}_{1/2}=2.3$ million years, we have\n\n$$\n2.3=\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{k}\\,⇒\\,k=\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{2.3}.\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\nM(t)={M}_{0}{e}^{-t\\operatorname{ln}⁡2/2.3}.\n$$\n\nWe wish to calculate the time $t$ such that $M(t)=0.42{M}_{0}.$ So, we set up our equation as follows:\n\n$$\n0.42{M}_{0}={M}_{0}{e}^{-t\\operatorname{ln}⁡2/2.3}\n$$\n\nSolving this equation for $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0.42{M}_{0} & ={M}_{0}{e}^{-t\\operatorname{ln}⁡2/2.3} \\\\ 0.42\\cancel{{M}_{0}} & =\\cancel{{M}_{0}}{e}^{-t\\operatorname{ln}⁡2/2.3} \\\\ 0.42 & ={e}^{-t\\operatorname{ln}⁡2/2.3} \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡(0.42) & =ln\\,({e}^{-t\\operatorname{ln}⁡2/2.3}) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡(0.42) & =-\\frac{t\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{2.3} \\\\ t & =-\\frac{2.3\\operatorname{ln}⁡(0.42)}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2} \\\\ t & ≈2.9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the sample is approximately $2.9$ million years old." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14047446/state.json" "file_sha256" "2683497908ddd9ff288e40508eaa66744ec80d4c4c87fb7e4bd164574ef440ed"} {"question" "q-355652" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14047446/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "6c55a0b8ef2d290be1baf4020ae915a1985f32a1a26249515158e73e63f0c004"} {"question" "q-355652" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14047446/state.json" "file_sha256" "2683497908ddd9ff288e40508eaa66744ec80d4c4c87fb7e4bd164574ef440ed"} {"question" "q-355652" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "2.9"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14047446/state.json" "file_sha256" "2683497908ddd9ff288e40508eaa66744ec80d4c4c87fb7e4bd164574ef440ed"} {"question" "e-9374" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The mass $M(t),$ in grams, of a sample of iron-60 is given by\n\n$$\nM(t) = 100 e^{-kt},\n$$\n\nwhere $t$ is the time, in millions of years. The half-life of iron-60 is $2.6$ million years. Rounded to three significant figures, what is the value of $k?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14047446/example-9374.json" "file_sha256" "34a25f425d6f8c554c69c5894d5f676ff2a2b8cc17bca328e5331adfbb49a418"} {"question" "e-9374" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The mass $M(t)$ of a radioactive substance at time $t$ can be modeled by the differential equation\n\n$$\nM'(t) = -kM(t), \\qquad M(0) = M_0.\n$$\n\nThe general solution to this equation is\n\n$$\nM(t) = M_0e^{-kt}.\n$$\n\nThe half-life $t_{1/2}$ is the time taken for the mass of the substance to reduce by one half and is given by\n\n$$\nt_{1/2} = \\dfrac{\\ln 2}{k}.\n$$\n\nSince we know that $t_{1/2} = 2.6$ million years, we have\n\n$$\n2.6 = \\dfrac{\\ln 2}{k}\\quad\\Rightarrow\\quad k = \\dfrac{\\ln 2}{2.6} \\approx 0.267.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14047446/example-9374.json" "file_sha256" "34a25f425d6f8c554c69c5894d5f676ff2a2b8cc17bca328e5331adfbb49a418"} {"question" "e-6675" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The half-life of bismuth-210 is $5.012$ days. How long does it take for a $1\\,280\\,\\textrm g$ sample of bismuth-210 to reduce to $5\\,\\textrm g?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14047446/example-6675.json" "file_sha256" "b7d57ee6e92639ffd0f4ba2e0105b6dbcb7987f1750b8cbb58ef559411249c9e"} {"question" "e-6675" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The mass $M(t)$ of a radioactive substance at time $t$ can be modeled by the differential equation\n\n$$\nM'(t) = -kM(t), \\qquad M(0) = M_0.\n$$\n\nThe general solution to this equation is\n\n$$\nM(t) = M_0e^{-kt}.\n$$\n\nThe half-life $t_{1/2}$ is the time taken for the mass of the substance to reduce by one half and is given by\n\n$$\nt_{1/2} = \\dfrac{\\ln 2}{k}.\n$$\n\nThere are two methods that we can use to solve this problem. We describe each of them below.\n\n**Method 1**\n\nSince we know that $t_{1/2} = 5.012$ days we have\n\n$$\n5.012 = \\dfrac{\\ln 2}{k}\\quad\\Rightarrow\\quad k = \\dfrac{\\ln 2}{5.012}\n$$\n\nAlso, since $M_0 = 1\\,280\\,\\textrm g,$ we have the particular solution\n\n$$\nM(t) = 1\\,280 e^{-t\\ln 2/5.012}.\n$$\n\nWe wish to calculate the time $t$ such that $M(t) = 5\\,\\textrm g.$ So, we set up our equation as follows:\n\n$$\n5 = 1\\,280 e^{-t\\ln 2/5.012}\n$$\n\nSolving this equation for $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5 & =1\\,280{e}^{-t\\operatorname{ln}⁡2/5.012} \\\\ \\frac{5}{1\\,280} & ={e}^{-t\\operatorname{ln}⁡2/5.012} \\\\ \\frac{1}{256} & ={e}^{-t\\operatorname{ln}⁡2/5.012} \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{1}{256}) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{-t\\operatorname{ln}⁡2/5.012}) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡1-\\operatorname{ln}⁡256 & =-\\frac{t\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{5.012} \\\\ -\\operatorname{ln}⁡256 & =-\\frac{t\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{5.012} \\\\ t & =\\frac{5.012\\cdot \\operatorname{ln}⁡256}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2} \\\\ t & ≈40.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that it takes approximately $40$ days for the mass of the substance to reduce to $5\\,\\textrm g.$\n\n**Method 2**\n\nWe wish to calculate the time $t$ such that $M(t) = 5\\,\\textrm g.$ Notice that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{M(t)}{M(0)} & =\\frac{5\\,\\text{g}}{1\\,280\\,\\text{g}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{256} \\\\  & ={(\\frac{1}{2})}^{8},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nor equivalently\n\n$$\nM(t) =  \\left(\\dfrac{1}{2}\\right)^8\\cdot M(0).\n$$\n\nFrom this equation, we see that $8$ half-lives must have occurred for the mass to reduce from $1\\,280\\,\\textrm g$ to $5\\,\\textrm g.$ Hence,\n\n$$\nt = 8\\cdot t_{1/2} =8 \\cdot 5.012 \\approx 40.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that it takes approximately $40$ days for the mass of the substance to reduce to $5\\,\\textrm g.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14047446/example-6675.json" "file_sha256" "b7d57ee6e92639ffd0f4ba2e0105b6dbcb7987f1750b8cbb58ef559411249c9e"} {"question" "e-9373" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "At a geological site, an object is found with $65\\%$ of its original chlorine-36 content. Given that the half-life of chlorine-36 is $301$ thousand years, approximately how old is the object?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14047446/example-9373.json" "file_sha256" "8538e67e5e516d237d604a790734c75e21df12672fe8bb499328279c9df1e46f"} {"question" "e-9373" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The mass $M(t)$ of a radioactive substance at time $t$ can be modeled by the differential equation\n\n$$\nM'(t) = -kM(t), \\qquad M(0) = M_0.\n$$\n\nThe general solution to this equation is\n\n$$\nM(t) = M_0e^{-kt}.\n$$\n\nThe half-life $t_{1/2}$ is the time taken for the mass of the substance to reduce by one half and is given by\n\n$$\nt_{1/2} = \\dfrac{\\ln 2}{k}.\n$$\n\nSince we know that $t_{1/2} = 301$ thousand years, we have\n\n$$\n301 = \\dfrac{\\ln 2}{k}\\quad\\Rightarrow\\quad k = \\dfrac{\\ln 2}{301}.\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\nM(t) = M_0 e^{-t\\ln 2/301}.\n$$\n\nWe wish to calculate the time $t$ such that $M(t) = 0.65 M_0.$ So, we set up our equation as follows:\n\n$$\n0.65M_0 = M_0 e^{-t\\ln 2/301}\n$$\n\nSolving this equation for $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0.65{M}_{0} & ={M}_{0}{e}^{-t\\operatorname{ln}⁡2/301} \\\\ 0.65\\cancel{{M}_{0}} & =\\cancel{{M}_{0}}{e}^{-t\\operatorname{ln}⁡2/301} \\\\ 0.65 & ={e}^{-t\\operatorname{ln}⁡2/301} \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡(0.65) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{-t\\operatorname{ln}⁡2/301}) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡(0.65) & =-\\frac{t\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{301} \\\\ t & =-\\frac{301\\cdot \\operatorname{ln}⁡(0.65)}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2} \\\\ t & ≈187.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the object is approximately $187$ thousand years old." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14047446/example-9373.json" "file_sha256" "8538e67e5e516d237d604a790734c75e21df12672fe8bb499328279c9df1e46f"}] "usage" {"e-9374" [] "e-9373" [] "e-6675" []} "basis" 1703 "retractions" [[17592186179487 :question/problem "The mass $M(t)$, in grams, of a sample of iron-60 is given by\n\n$M(t) = 100 e^{-kt}$,\n\nwhere $t$ is the time, in millions of years. The half-life of iron-60 is $2.6$ million years. Rounded to three significant figures, what is the value of $k$?"] [17592186179487 :question/worked-solution "The mass $M(t)$ of a radioactive substance at time $t$ can be modeled by the differential equation\n\n$$\nM'(t) = -kM(t), \\qquad M(0) = M_0\n$$\n\nThe general solution to this equation is\n\n$$\nM(t) = M_0e^{-kt}\n$$\n\nThe half-life $t_{1/2}$ is the time taken for the mass of the substance to reduce by one half and is given by\n\n$$\nt_{1/2} = \\dfrac{\\ln 2}{k}\n$$\n\nSince we know that\n\n$$\nt_{1/2} = 2.6\n$$\n\nmillion years, we have\n\n$$\n2.6 = \\dfrac{\\ln 2}{k}\\quad\\Rightarrow\\quad k = \\dfrac{\\ln 2}{2.6} \\approx 0.267\n$$"] [17592186179458 :question/problem "The half-life of bismuth-210 is $5.012$ days. How long does it take for a $1\\,280\\,\\textrm g$ sample of bismuth-210 to reduce to $5\\,\\textrm g$?"] [17592186179458 :question/worked-solution "The mass $M(t)$ of a radioactive substance at time $t$ can be modeled by the differential equation\n\n$$\nM'(t) = -kM(t), \\qquad M(0) = M_0\n$$\n\nThe general solution to this equation is\n\n$$\nM(t) = M_0e^{-kt}\n$$\n\nThe half-life $t_{1/2}$ is the time taken for the mass of the substance to reduce by one half and is given by\n\n$$\nt_{1/2} = \\dfrac{\\ln 2}{k}\n$$\n\nThere are two methods that we can use to solve this problem. We describe each of them below.\n\n**Method 1**\n\nSince we know that\n\n$$\nt_{1/2} = 5.012\n$$\n\ndays we have\n\n$$\n5.012 = \\dfrac{\\ln 2}{k}\\quad\\Rightarrow\\quad k = \\dfrac{\\ln 2}{5.012}\n$$\n\nAlso, since\n\n$$\nM_0 = 1\\,280\\,\\textrm g\n$$\n\nwe have the particular solution\n\n$$\nM(t) = 1\\,280 e^{-t\\ln 2/5.012}\n$$\n\nWe wish to calculate the time $t$ such that\n\n$$\nM(t) = 5\\,\\textrm g\n$$\n\nSo, we set up our equation as follows:\n\n$$\n5 = 1\\,280 e^{-t\\ln 2/5.012}\n$$\n\nSolving this equation for $t$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n5 &= 1280e^{-t\\ln 2/5.012} \\\\\n\\frac{5}{1280} &= e^{-t\\ln 2/5.012} \\\\\n\\frac{1}{256} &= e^{-t\\ln 2/5.012} \\\\\n\\ln (\\frac{1}{256}) &= \\ln (e^{-t\\ln 2/5.012}) \\\\\n\\ln 1 - \\ln 256 &= -\\frac{t\\ln 2}{5.012} \\\\\n- \\ln 256 &= -\\frac{t\\ln 2}{5.012} \\\\\nt &= \\frac{5.012 \\cdot \\ln 256}{\\ln 2} \\\\\n&= t \\mid \\approx 40\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that it takes approximately $40$ days for the mass of the substance to reduce to $5\\,\\textrm g$.\n\n**Method 2**\n\nWe wish to calculate the time $t$ such that\n\n$$\nM(t) = 5\\,\\textrm g\n$$\n\nNotice that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(M(t))/(M(0)) &= \\frac{5g}{1280g} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{256} \\\\\n&= (\\frac{1}{2})^{8}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nor equivalently\n\n$$\nM(t) = \\left(\\dfrac{1}{2}\\right)^8\\cdot M(0)\n$$\n\nFrom this equation, we see that $8$ half-lives must have occurred for the mass to reduce from $1\\,280\\,\\textrm g$ to $5\\,\\textrm g$. Hence,\n\n$$\nt = 8\\cdot t_{1/2} =8 \\cdot 5.012 \\approx 40\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that it takes approximately $40$ days for the mass of the substance to reduce to $5\\,\\textrm g$."] [17592186179486 :question/worked-solution "The mass $M(t)$ of a radioactive substance at time $t$ can be modeled by the differential equation\n\n$$\nM'(t) = -kM(t), \\qquad M(0) = M_0\n$$\n\nThe general solution to this equation is\n\n$$\nM(t) = M_0e^{-kt}\n$$\n\nThe half-life $t_{1/2}$ is the time taken for the mass of the substance to reduce by one half and is given by\n\n$$\nt_{1/2} = \\dfrac{\\ln 2}{k}\n$$\n\nSince we know that\n\n$$\nt_{1/2} = 301\n$$\n\nthousand years, we have\n\n$$\n301 = \\dfrac{\\ln 2}{k}\\quad\\Rightarrow\\quad k = \\dfrac{\\ln 2}{301}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\nM(t) = M_0 e^{-t\\ln 2/301}\n$$\n\nWe wish to calculate the time $t$ such that\n\n$$\nM(t) = 0.65 M_0\n$$\n\nSo, we set up our equation as follows:\n\n$$\n0.65M_0 = M_0 e^{-t\\ln 2/301}\n$$\n\nSolving this equation for $t$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n0.65M_{0} &= M_{0}e^{-t\\ln 2/301} \\\\\n0.65M_{0} &= M_{0}e^{-t\\ln 2/301} \\\\\n0.65 &= e^{-t\\ln 2/301} \\\\\n\\ln (0.65) &= \\ln (e^{-t\\ln 2/301}) \\\\\n\\ln (0.65) &= -\\frac{t\\ln 2}{301} \\\\\nt &= - (301 \\cdot \\ln (0.65))/(\\ln 2) \\\\\n&= t \\mid \\approx 187\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the object is approximately $187$ thousand years old."]] "immutable_answers" [] "immutable_fields" [] "no_history" []}
